blockd/tests/golden_root_cost.rs, release, aarch64 Linux container (18 logical
cpus, Docker volume for the target dir), median of 3, local file object store.
Same platform as raw/commit-cost-delta-pack.log so the two changes are comparable.

Same process, same object store, same volume geometry -- the only variable is how
a volume gets its rootfs:

  copy      = stream the image into the volume and commit it (what a create did)
  reference = publish the image once, provision by referencing its chunks (ADR-0017)

One create:

 image MiB       path uploaded MiB    objects provision ms    speedup        gen
        64       copy        64.00         19        187.8       1.0x          1
        64  reference         0.00          3          6.5      28.9x          1
        64  (publish)         once          -        456.5          -          -
       256       copy       256.01         67        815.1       1.0x          1
       256  reference         0.01          3          7.5     108.4x          1
       256  (publish)         once          -       1762.4          -          -
       512       copy       512.01        131       1239.8       1.0x          1
       512  reference         0.02          3          7.0     178.3x          1
       512  (publish)         once          -       2732.7          -          -

N Cats from one image at once (256 MiB image):

    cats       path uploaded MiB    objects        wall ms    speedup
       1       copy       256.01         67          700.1       1.0x
       1  reference         0.01          3            7.0      99.6x
       4       copy      1024.02        268         2130.7       1.0x
       4  reference         0.04         12           17.4     122.7x
      10       copy      2560.05        670         5725.1       1.0x
      10  reference         0.11         30           56.0     102.2x

Cold read of every chunk on a host with an empty cache (64 MiB):

        path       cold read ms              MiB/s
        copy              143.5                446
   reference              141.5                452

Reading:

* Provisioning cost is independent of image size (~7 ms): it writes map nodes and
  a commit record and nothing else. The 0.01-0.02 MiB "uploaded" is those nodes.
* Concurrent creates no longer contend on the object store at all: 10 Cats upload
  0.11 MiB between them instead of 2.5 GiB.
* Referencing a shared chunk costs nothing to read -- a provisioned chunk is one
  object exactly like a private one (446 vs 452 MiB/s, i.e. within noise, with the
  referenced side marginally ahead).
* The `copy` column is the engine-level copy, not the old path that streamed the
  image through an NBD socket, so these speedups are conservative.
* Publishing costs ~2.7 s for 512 MiB and happens once per image digest. Today the
  first launch that uses an image pays it.

macOS arm64 (APFS) for a second platform: raw/golden-root-cost-macos.log. Same
shape, smaller multiples (124x single / 38x for 10 concurrent) because APFS makes
the `copy` side cheaper relative to overlayfs in the container.

Also found by this bench: `ChunkEngine::detach` joined a lease-renewal thread that
was sleeping out lease_ttl_ms/3, so every detach -- every stop, suspend and cold
migration, per volume -- waited ~1.67 s at the default TTL. Before the fix every
row above had a ~1674 ms floor, which is what made it visible. Renewal now waits on
a condvar; regression test:
engine::tests::detaching_does_not_wait_out_the_lease_renewal_interval.
